/**
 * How many synched-data accessors each entity class declares.
 *
 * <p><b>Generated by tools/gen_entity_data_counts.py - do not edit by hand.</b> The counts
 * are read out of decompiled-1.18.2-reference by counting SynchedEntityData.defineId call
 * sites per class literal, so they cannot drift from vanilla without the generator saying
 * so. Regenerate after changing anything that declares an accessor.
 *
 * <p>This exists to make {@code defineId} independent of class-initialisation order. Its
 * numbering walks up to the nearest ancestor that has <i>already initialised</i> and
 * continues from there, which assumes superclasses initialise before subclasses - a JVM
 * guarantee TeaVM does not make. When Sheep initialised before AgeableMob the walk stepped
 * over AgeableMob to Mob and numbered DATA_WOOL_ID 16 instead of 17; AgeableMob then took
 * 16 too, and every sheep in the world failed to spawn with "Duplicate id value for 16!".
 *
 * <p>With these counts the base id is arithmetic over the static hierarchy instead:
 * {@link #baseIdFor} sums the counts of every strict ancestor, which is the same number
 * vanilla arrives at and needs only {@code getSuperclass()}. Order stops mattering.
 *
 * <p>These ids travel on the wire, so they are not free to be merely self-consistent -
 * they have to be vanilla's, or entity metadata is misread by any real server this
 * connects to. tools/VerifyEntityDataIds.java checks them against a desktop JVM.
 */
